counterfactuals.output:
Combined HST UV/optical imaging of the crowded core and wide-field ground-based data produced a nearly complete catalog of roughly 60 bright blue straggler stars (BSSs) in NGC 5904 (M5). The ultraviolet images were especially effective at separating hot BSSs from the numerous cool giants in the cluster center.

The principal result was that the BSS radial distribution is strongly bimodal when compared with normal reference populations such as horizontal-branch and red-giant stars, or with the sampled cluster light:

- a pronounced central concentration of BSSs;
- a sharp decline to a minimum at intermediate radii, around several to roughly ten core radii;
- a renewed increase in the BSS specific frequency in the outer cluster.

This pattern is interpreted dynamically. Massive BSSs and their binary progenitors in the inner regions have sunk toward the core through dynamical friction, producing the central peak and depleting the intermediate zone. In the low-density outskirts, dynamical friction has not yet acted efficiently, so BSSs formed mainly through mass transfer or mergers in primordial binaries remain there, producing the outer upturn. The central population may also include BSSs formed or modified through stellar collisions and binary interactions. Thus M5’s BSS distribution provides evidence for both core dynamical processing and an undisturbed outer binary population, and marks the cluster as dynamically evolved but not yet dynamically relaxed over its full radial extent.
